pposite place.\n\nNow, let's move to the next part of the problem: we're dealing with $(1+i)^{10}$:\n\n- Using the binomial theorem, expand $(1+i)^{10} = (1 + 1\\cdot i)^{10} = 1^{10} + \\binom{10}{1}1^9 i + \\binom{10}{2}1^8i^2 + \\binom{10}{3}1^7 i^3 + \\ldots$.\n\n- Since $\\binom{10}{k} \\cdot i^k$ for every $k \\neq 1$ will result in the product of non-conjugate terms, their terms will cancel out when we consider the absolute values of these terms.\n\n- As such, all these terms become $0$ when taken in absolute value. We are left with $|1^{10} + \\binom{10}{1}1^9 i| = |1 + 10i| = \\sqrt{1^2 + 10^2} = \\sqrt{101}$.\n\n- Finally, applying this, the absolute value of $(1+i)^{10}$ is $\\sqrt{101}$.\n\nLet's check by calculating $(1+i)^{10}$ and the product with $5^{-10}$ to ensure our understanding from the problem is accurate:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n&[(1+i)^{10}]^2 \\\\\n= & [(1+10i+54i^2+145i^3+292i^4+315i^5+261i^6+155i^7+50i^8+i^{10})]^2 \\\\\n= & [(1+10i-54-145i-292+315i+261-155+50+0)^2 + (1+10i+54i^2+145i^3+292i^4+315i^5+261i^6+155i^7+50i^8 + i^{10})^2] \\\\\n= & [(-75+41i)^2 + (1+10i-54+54i-145-145i-292+292-120-40-64 + i^{10})^2] \\\\\n= & [8625-620i-2215+2401i-11250+2324+410i-1695-3056+640-1040+64-(2+2i)^2] \\\\\n= & [3545+721i-9921+410i-3211+4338-3992+1056-1040-52+144+\\boxed{35-69i}].\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nIf we ignore complex part and use the ACC method, the final result is:\n- When incorporating the absolute values of this and the original problem, so we get \n$$|A|^2 + 5^{-10}, $$\n\n\n\nWhere, $A$ is one of these.\n- $|A| = \\sqrt{(1+30+60+35-12)^2 - (2+ 30+18+28+9+32-12)^2)} = 32.0$\n\n- $|A| = \\sqrt{(1+20+40+21-12)^2 - (2+20+22+41+9+32-12)^2)} = -4\\sqrt{76} $,\n\n- $A = \\bot $\n\n- $A = \\bot $.\n\nThus, the sum of absolute values s ous up is $\\boxed{880}$.\n\nWe can use SymPy to calculate the dot product and the magnitude:\n\nThis solution uses the `dot` and `norm` functions from SymPy.\n\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, I, Abs, sqrt, °